From: mayipashu <mayipashu@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 05:30:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: npu: publish the mailbox command before the
 doorbell

The mailbox command word (CTRL3) carries the function slot in bits 14:11
and a "wait for completion" bit 0 that asks the firmware to set the done
bit once its handler returns. The firmware reads CTRL3 from inside its
mailbox ISR, and the ISR fires on the doorbell counter bump (CTRL2).

The driver wrote CTRL0/CTRL1, bumped CTRL2, and only then wrote CTRL3. If
the firmware handles the interrupt before the host reaches the CTRL3
store, it sees the previous message's argument, finds bit 0 clear, and
takes the "notify the host" path instead of completing the request, so
DONE is never set and the host times out.

Observed on H3C HM2004-DU (AN7581 + MT7916) with the ClankerNPU firmware:
the NPU finished bring-up (MIB31 == 0xCCCCCCCC) and printed its banner,
but every host request timed out, including the WLAN version query.

Write CTRL3 first and ring the doorbell last, so the ISR always sees a
fully described message. The register definitions are unchanged: both
sides already agree on bits 14:11 for the slot, bit 0 for the wait, bit 1
for done and bits 4:2 for the handler return value.

Signed-off-by: mayipashu <mayipashu@users.noreply.github.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_npu.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_npu.c
@@ -183,7 +183,7 @@
 				 u16 reply_len)
 {
 	struct airoha_npu_core *core = &npu->cores[0]; /* FIXME: core */
-	u32 val, offset = 0;
+	u32 val, cmd, offset = 0;
 	int ret;
 
 	if (len > AIROHA_NPU_MBOX_SIZE)
@@ -206,10 +206,28 @@
 
 	regmap_write(npu->regmap, REG_CR_MBQ0_CTRL(0) + offset, core->addr);
 	regmap_write(npu->regmap, REG_CR_MBQ0_CTRL(1) + offset, len);
+
+	/*
+	 * Publish the command before ringing the doorbell.
+	 *
+	 * The firmware reads CTRL3 from its mailbox ISR: bits 14:11 pick the
+	 * function slot and bit 0 asks it to set the done bit when the
+	 * handler is finished. Bumping the doorbell first lets the ISR run
+	 * while CTRL3 still holds the previous message's argument (no wait
+	 * bit), so it takes the "notify the host" path instead and DONE is
+	 * never set. The host then reports a timeout for a message the
+	 * firmware did receive.
+	 *
+	 * Observed on H3C HM2004-DU with the ClankerNPU image: the NPU had
+	 * finished bring-up (MIB31 == 0xCCCCCCCC) and printed its banner,
+	 * yet every host request timed out, including the version query.
+	 */
+	cmd = FIELD_PREP(MBOX_MSG_FUNC_ID, func_id) | MBOX_MSG_WAIT_RSP;
+	regmap_write(npu->regmap, REG_CR_MBQ0_CTRL(3) + offset, cmd);
+
+	/* ring the doorbell last, once the message is fully described */
 	regmap_read(npu->regmap, REG_CR_MBQ0_CTRL(2) + offset, &val);
 	regmap_write(npu->regmap, REG_CR_MBQ0_CTRL(2) + offset, val + 1);
-	val = FIELD_PREP(MBOX_MSG_FUNC_ID, func_id) | MBOX_MSG_WAIT_RSP;
-	regmap_write(npu->regmap, REG_CR_MBQ0_CTRL(3) + offset, val);
 
 	ret = regmap_read_poll_timeout_atomic(npu->regmap,
 					      REG_CR_MBQ0_CTRL(3) + offset,
